Title: Diving Deeper into Subregular Syntax
Author: Thomas Graf
Date: 2026-03-15
Category: Papers
Tags: Minimalist grammars; first-order logic; formal grammar; islands; subregular; syntax

<div markdown class="authors">
Graf, Thomas
</div>

<div markdown class="abstract">
<span id="abstract-title">Abstract</span>
In my target paper Subregular linguistics: Bridging theoretical linguistics and formal grammar I argued that theoretical linguistics and formal grammar, despite major differences in methodology, have a lot to offer to each other, and that the recently emerged subfield of subregular linguistics provides a conduit for knowledge transfer between the two.
I thank all the authors for their thought-provoking commentaries that greatly enrich the discussion that I hoped to start with the initial paper.
Here I focus on some shared themes revolving around subregular syntax: the role of mappings, with Irish wh-agreement as a concrete example; the challenges of linearization, with a new Generalized Ban on Improper Movement derived from subregular limits of linearization; the importance of careful empirical analysis, including a brief discussion of existing work on gradient TSL and its empirical applications; and finally, what exactly it means to say that a specific phenomenon falls within a subregular class like TSL, and why this is orthogonal to the issue of formalisms and metalanguages.
</div>

